  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\n- Periods of showers and storms Sunday afternoon into Monday\n\nWe are seeing some slightly cooler and drier air filter over the \narea this afternoon in the wake of a cold frontal passage earlier \ntoday. This will remain the case through Sunday morning.\n\nWe will see shower/storm chances increase from West to East across \nthe area Sunday afternoon. A majority of model data shows a complex \nof showers and storms coming in after 18z for most of the \nsouthern two thirds of the forecast area. \n\nThese will be supported by a low level jet that will be weakening as \nthe nose of it moves toward the area. In addition, these \nshowers/storms will be moving toward an area that is much less \nunstable due to the air mass replacement that took place today. MU \nCAPEs are forecast to be less than 1000 J/kg by late afternoon, with \na stronger gradient of instability to our south. These factors will \nsignificantly limit strong to severe storms Sunday afternoon.\n\nWe expect additional showers/storms to develop to our SW Sunday \nevening as another low level jet ramps up with another MCV/short \nwave that will be riding along the instability gradient. The window \nbetween the afternoon and evening storms may be so short, that \noutflow boundaries, or just the tail of the storms might be acted \nupon by the new low level jet. \n\nThe significance of this could be that we see backbuilding off of \nthe first line of storms, that could lead to some training of storms \nand a potential flooding threat. A flooding threat looks more \npossible up in our area over the southern counties, than a severe \nthreat, as the instability is more elevated, and the forcing from \nthe low level jet will be focused over our extreme southern area, \nand further south. Precipitable water values look to max out just \nsouth of our area too just short of 2.0\". It is still very healthy \nover much of our area being around 1.8\" or so.\n\nThis is all likely to push south of the area by Monday afternoon the \nlow level jet moves by, and likely pushes the best moisture axis \njust south of the area. The models seem to be trying to pop some \ndiurnal showers/storms Monday afternoon with much of the area seeing \n1000-1500 J/kg of CAPE present. \n\n- Mainly small daytime shower/storm chances Tuesday and Wednesday\n\nThe edge of the heat dome (and best instability) will sink a bit \nfurther south by Tuesday and Wednesday, but we do not see a major \nchange in air masses for our area. Temperatures will still max out \nin the 80s mid week, and dew points will remain in the 60s. We will \nbe a bit closer to the jet stream, with weak short waves potentially \nsparking a few showers/storms off mainly during the afternoon and \nevening hours. \n\n- Cooler late week with returning chances of rain\n\nThe threat of rain looks to almost disappear for a couple of days \nfor Thursday and Friday. It is at that time that we see high \npressure over Ontario bring in a flow from the NE that dries us out \na bit more. This will take away lingering instability from Tue/Wed, \nwith a short wave ridge also helping to keep us dry.\n\nThe dry weather potential looks to remain short lived as we will see \nthe upper flow become from the SW. This will try to push the edge of \nthe heat dome toward the area again, with short waves riding along \nthe flow that could tough off showers and storms beginning as early \nas Friday night into Saturday.",
